⏺ Great, I now have a comprehensive picture. Here are my ideas, roughly ordered from most impactful/practical to more speculative:

  ---
  1. Extract a DamageSystem subsystem

  The biggest win. 14 fields and ~25 methods are purely about damage modification (prevention shields, color/type prevention, prevention rules, 4 different redirect types, reflection, reverse damage, forcefield,
  bodyguard, combat damage prevention). This is nearly half the EffectManager by line count.

  type DamageSystem struct {
      regenerationShields    map[uuid.UUID]int
      preventionShields      map[uuid.UUID]int
      preventionRules        []damagePreventionRule
      colorPrevention        map[uuid.UUID][]Color
      typePrevention         map[uuid.UUID][]CardType
      reverseDamageShields   map[uuid.UUID]bool
      forcefieldShields      map[uuid.UUID]bool
      preventCombatDamage    bool
      bodyguard              map[uuid.UUID]uuid.UUID
      playerDamageRedirect   map[uuid.UUID]uuid.UUID
      artifactDamageRedirect map[uuid.UUID]uuid.UUID
      creatureDamageRedirect map[uuid.UUID]uuid.UUID
      damageReflection       map[uuid.UUID]damageReflectionEntry
  }

  Access becomes g.Effects.Damage.PreventDamage(...) instead of g.Effects.PreventDamage(...). The DamageSystem owns its own ResetPerCycle() and ClearEndOfTurn() methods, which Apply() calls. This makes the damage
  pipeline legible as a standalone unit — and it's a natural boundary because damage resolution in MTG is already a self-contained algorithm.

  ---
  2. Declarative lifecycle management instead of manual resets

  Right now Apply() has ~20 lines of manual resets, and EOT cleanup is scattered across game.go. Fields fall into clear lifecycle categories but there's no enforcement — forgetting to reset a new field is a silent
  bug.

  Idea: tag fields by lifecycle and automate cleanup:

  type trackedState[T any] struct {
      value    T
      lifetime Lifetime // PerCycle, EndOfTurn, EndOfCombat, Indefinite, OneShot
      reset    func(*T) // e.g., func(m *map[uuid.UUID]bool) { clear(*m) }
  }

  Then Apply(), RemoveEndOfTurn(), and RemoveEndOfCombat() each just iterate the tracked states matching their lifetime. Adding a new field means declaring it with a lifetime — the cleanup is automatic. This
  eliminates a whole class of bugs.

  A lighter-weight version: just have resetPerCycle(), clearEndOfTurn(), clearEndOfCombat() methods on each subsystem, and Apply() calls them. Less magical, still organized.

  ---
  3. Unify the redirect/shield hierarchy into a damage pipeline

  Currently, game.go's damage resolution code manually checks 7+ effect manager fields in a specific order. That ordering logic lives in game.go, not in the effect system. If you extract a DamageSystem, you can give
  it a single entry point:

  func (ds *DamageSystem) ResolveDamage(ctx DamageContext) DamageResult {
      // 1. Check prevention rules
      // 2. Check color/type prevention
      // 3. Apply prevention shields
      // 4. Check redirects (bodyguard, player, artifact, creature)
      // 5. Check reflection
      // 6. Check forcefield
      // 7. Check reverse damage
      // 8. Apply remaining damage
  }

  This turns the implicit damage pipeline in game.go into an explicit, testable pipeline inside the damage system. Each step is a method you can unit test in isolation. game.go just calls ResolveDamage().

  ---
  4. Extract GameRules as a first-class query object

  The gameRuleModifiers nested struct (mana conversion, spell costs, untap limits, hand size, land plays, expansion blocks, Channel, Sanctuary, Lich, min life, skip draw) is really a "what are the current game
  rules?" query surface. Extract it:

  type GameRules struct { ... }

  func (r *GameRules) CanCastFromExpansion(exp string) bool
  func (r *GameRules) SpellCostDelta(color Color) int  // increase - reduction
  func (r *GameRules) MaxLandUntaps() int
  func (r *GameRules) MaxHandSize(playerID uuid.UUID) int
  func (r *GameRules) IsChannelActive(playerID uuid.UUID) bool
  // etc.

  Access: g.Effects.Rules.MaxHandSize(playerID). Again, ResetPerCycle() and ClearEndOfTurn() methods. The game rules subsystem becomes independently testable.

  ---
  5. Replace one-off boolean/map flags with a generic StateToken system

  Several fields follow the exact same pattern: Set(id), Is/Has(id) bool, Clear(id). Examples: sanctuaryActive, channelActive, forcefieldShields, reverseDamageShields, minimumLife, lichActive, skipNextDraw.

  A generic building block:

  type PlayerFlags struct {
      flags map[uuid.UUID]map[StateFlag]bool
  }

  func (pf *PlayerFlags) Set(player uuid.UUID, flag StateFlag)
  func (pf *PlayerFlags) Has(player uuid.UUID, flag StateFlag) bool
  func (pf *PlayerFlags) Clear(player uuid.UUID, flag StateFlag)
  func (pf *PlayerFlags) ClearAll(flag StateFlag)

  Then channelActive, sanctuaryActive, minimumLife, forcefieldShields, reverseDamageShields all collapse into entries in one map. Fewer fields, less boilerplate, same semantics. The tradeoff is slightly less explicit
   naming at call sites — g.Effects.PlayerFlags.Has(pid, FlagChannel) vs g.Effects.IsChannelActive(pid) — but you could keep wrapper methods for readability.

  ---
  6. Move Lich, Channel, Sanctuary into card-registered replacement effects

  These are currently special-cased fields because they fundamentally alter game rules. But conceptually, they're replacement effects — Lich replaces life loss with card loss, Channel replaces mana payment with life
  payment, Sanctuary replaces the draw step.

  If the engine had a proper replacement effect system (which MTG formally defines), these cards would register replacements, and the engine would check g.Effects.ReplacementEffects(EventDrawStep) instead of
  g.Effects.ShouldSkipDraw(). This is a bigger architectural change, but it's the "correct" MTG rules model and would let future sets add replacement effects without touching EffectManager.

  ---
  7. Make Apply() more composable

  Currently Apply() is a monolithic 89-line method. If you extract subsystems (DamageSystem, GameRules), Apply becomes:

  func (em *EffectManager) Apply(g *Game) {
      em.damage.ResetPerCycle()
      em.rules.ResetPerCycle()
      em.resetAttrDeltas()
      em.resetPermanents(g)
      em.pruneStaleEffects(g)
      em.applyLayers(g)
      em.rules.SyncManaConversions(g)
      em.finalizeAttrs(g)
  }

  Each sub-step is a focused method. The orchestration reads like documentation.

  ---
  8. Move blockPairRestrictions to a combat-scoped context

  Block pair restrictions only matter during combat declaration. Instead of living permanently on EffectManager and being reset every cycle, they could be computed on-demand when the game asks "can this creature
  block that one?" — similar to how you'd check HasAttr(CantBeBlocked). A CanBlock(blocker, attacker, g *Game) bool method that evaluates continuous effects at query time instead of caching pairs.

